==English==

===Etymology===
First attested in English in 1509<ref name="OEtD-etymdate&etym1">''Online Etymology Dictionary'', © 2001 Douglas Harper</ref>; either:
* from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|brevitatem|lang=la}};<ref name="OEtD-etymdate&etym1"/> or
* from {{etyl|xno|en}} {{term|brevite|lang=xno}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|brievete|lang=fro}};<ref name="D.C-etym2">''Dictionary.com Unabridged'' (v 1·1)</ref>from {{etyl|la}} {{term|brevitas|brevitās|lang=la}}, from {{term|brevis||short|lang=la}}.<ref name="OEtD-etymdate&etym1"/><ref name="AHD-etym">''American Heritage Dictionary of the English Language'', Fourth Edition</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈbɹɛv.ɪ.ti/}}, {{X-SAMPA|/"brEv.I.ti/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|brevities}}

# {{uncountable}} The quality of being [[brief]] in duration.
#* {{quote-book
|year=2005
|author=Bill Bryson
|title=A short history of nearly everything
|passage= Thanks to Global Positioning Systems we can see that Europe and North America are parting at about the speed a fingernail grows—roughly two yards in a human lifetime. If you were prepared to wait long enough, you could ride from Los Angeles all the way up to San Francisco. It is only the [[brevity]] of lifetimes that keeps us from appreciating the changes.}}
# {{uncountable}} [[succinctness|Succinctness]]; [[conciseness]].
#* {{quote-book
|year=1966
|author=Jackson E. Morris
|title=Principles of scientific and technical writing
|passage=A good technical writing style will now be defined as a style possessing clarity, '''brevity''', and variety.}}
# {{rare|countable}} A short piece of writing.

